# mend.play — plan #17 `S6`: THE TOWN IS WHAT FIXES YOUR GEAR, AND IT COSTS NO KEY.
#
# `mendtest` states the algebra — damage is an event, repair is its exact inverse, and the
# condition rides the item through every seam. What it CANNOT say is that any of this is
# reachable in the world a player boots into, because it runs on `sim_new()` sandboxes.
#
# This script is that claim, on `story.loft`'s own GEN_SEED, in the town at depth 0:
#   • a SMITH stands in the shipped settlement, findable, alive, at the market seat;
#   • bumping them is the whole interface — `sim_talk_to`, the same seam that already
#     carries the bounty and the Circle's favour, so the repair costs ZERO new keys
#     (DESIGN §3a #7, and I-SAFE's zero-new-keys claim survives);
#   • the smith reads the gear, mends ONE piece, and says so;
#   • asked again with nothing wrong, they say that instead — the negative half, without
#     which "the smith answered" proves only that an NPC exists.
#
# ⚠ `corrode` IS A POKE AND THE ONLY ONE. The creature that carries the blow (a green jelly,
# MF_CORRODE) lives at depth 4; walking a level-1 hero down to meet one and back up is a
# different test, and `mendtest` row 1 already proves the blow wires to the melee arm. What
# is under test here is everything the settlement does afterwards.

genesis 1337 0
echo --- the shipped world, the home town, day one

expect depth == 0
expect alive == 1
remember ac0 ac

# Nothing is wrong yet, and the smith must say so rather than mend thin air. This row is
# also what makes the mend below mean something: a smith who answered identically either
# way would prove nothing about reading the gear.
expect damaged == 0
talk smith
expect damaged == 0

echo --- something ate the armour (a poke; the jelly is four levels down)
corrode
expect damaged == 1
expect ac < ac0

# ⚠ AND FIRST THE ANSWER I-SAFE IS FOR: A TOWN WITH NO IRON CANNOT HELP YOU.
# The forge lines are armed (the miners deliver from the ore face), so the smith draws the
# same store the forge draws — and at genesis that store is empty. This is the moment the
# whole plan closes on the PLAYER rather than on the town: an unsafe valley eventually means
# YOUR sword stays chipped.
echo --- the store is empty, and the smith says so
expect forgea == 0
talk smith
expect damaged == 1

echo --- the ore comes down (a poke; the miners' walk is stocktest's claim)
stock forge_armor 1
expect forgea == 1

echo --- NOW the town can answer: bump the smith, and it is mended
talk smith
expect damaged == 0
expect ac == ac0
expect forgea == 0

# ⚠ AND THE HP POOL CAME BACK WITH IT. Armour grants max HP, so a repair that restored AC
# alone would shave the character a little on every visit — invisible, permanent, and
# exactly the kind of drift a round-trip claim is for.
expect hp <= hpmax

echo --- and the smith has nothing left to do
talk smith
expect damaged == 0
expect ac == ac0
